# Brief

@hero
A strict markup language for documents
written by humans and consumed by humans _and_ LLMs.

@cta(href: "/getting-started/installation.html", kind: primary)[Get started] @cta(href: "https://github.com/kirillleventcov/brief", kind: secondary)[GitHub]
@end

@lede
Brief refuses ambiguity. Every construct has one canonical spelling, every
error has a code, and every document compiles to a deterministic,
token-minimized form for LLM prompts.
@end

## What is Brief?

Brief is a markup language designed to replace Markdown for documents that
are written by humans and consumed by *both* humans and LLMs. It targets
three goals that mainstream Markdown does not:

@features
@feature(title: "One way to do it")
Every construct has a single canonical spelling. `*bold*`, not `**bold**`.
`-` for bullets, never `*` or `+`. Two surface forms for the same AST is a
compile error.
@end

@feature(title: "Compiler-time errors")
Ambiguous input is a compile error with a source span and an error code, in
the style of `rustc`. There are no warnings — just success or failure.
@end

@feature(title: "Token economy")
`brief compile doc.brf --target=llm` produces a deterministic,
token-minimized rendering for LLM prompts. JSON code blocks minify by
default; more languages follow.
@end

@feature(title: "No inline HTML")
The single extension point is registered shortcodes. They cover what inline
HTML used to. Anything else means the document shouldn't be HTML in the
first place.
@end
@end

## A quick taste

```brief
# Release notes

The 0.4 release ships *strict* lists, _italic_ that doesn't fight you,
and a real compiler.

- [x] ship v0.4
- [ ] write release notes
- plain bullet, not a task

@callout(kind: tip)
Press @kbd[Ctrl+C] to abort the long-running compile.
@end

Some claim Brief saves tokens.@footnote[Measured against Markdown
on the project's own docs, roughly 25 percent on average.] The
compiler can prove it.
```

That is the language. There are no surprises. The full grammar reference
is on @ref[language/headings.brf](Headings) and the pages that follow.

## Markdown vs. Brief

Same content, two languages.

@compare
@col(label: "Markdown")
```markdown
# Title
**bold**, *italic*, ~~strike~~
- one
* two
+ three
```
@end
@col(label: "Brief")
```brief
# Title
*bold*, _italic_, ~strike~
- one
- two
- three
```
@end
@end

Brief refuses the second and third bullet markers. Pick one and use it.

## Errors look like rustc

Ambiguous input is a compile error, with a source span and an error code:

@diagnostic
```text
error[B0204]: emphasis cannot nest with the same marker
  --> doc.brf:14:23
   |
14 | This is *outer *inner* outer*.
   |        ^^^^^^^ inner emphasis re-uses the `*` marker
   |
   = help: use a different emphasis marker for the inner span,
           e.g. _outer *inner* outer_
```
@end

Run `brief explain B0204` for the long-form explanation of any error code.

